Context: in these notes Grothendieck axiomatizes a 'tame topology'. A notion of tame ('modérée') subsets of the spaces R^I is governed by axioms M1–M8. Earlier pages build tame spaces, tame embeddings and gluings; the previous page glues X_1 and X_2 along Z via embeddings into R^I × R^{A_1} × R^{A_2}.

This page, section IV, introduces strict ind-tame spaces. The data are:
- a set E;
- a family M_E of subsets of E (the 'tame parts'), stable under finite unions and covering E;
- a tame-space structure on each member of M_E.
Two conditions are imposed:
- the structures induce one another on overlaps;
- the family is hereditary: if A ∈ M and B is a tame part of A (B ∈ M_A), then B ∈ M.
In modern terms this is a set presented as a filtered colimit, along tame closed or locally tame embeddings, of tame spaces. It is the 'strict ind-object' version of a tame space.

Grothendieck next wants a notion of tame map from a tame X into such an E, under some 'usual' conditions. He then imposes axiom (M8): R is the union of its tame parts, filtered by M5. A natural example is R as the increasing union of bounded intervals when tame sets are, say, bounded semialgebraic or definable. Under M8, R, and likewise each R^I, gets a canonical ind-tame structure. This presumably serves to treat R^I and non-compact or non-bounded spaces inside a framework whose basic objects are 'bounded/compact' tame sets.

Proposition: the category Esp_M of ind-tame spaces has finite limits, and the forgetful functors (to sets, presumably also to the underlying space) commute with them. If R_0 ≠ ∅ (a nonemptiness or non-degeneracy hypothesis whose exact meaning is unclear from the page), Esp_M has finite direct sums and X ↦ |X| commutes with them.

The next page continues with:
- locally tame parts (A ∩ Y tame for all tame A);
- the stability properties of locally tame parts;
- quotients X/Y and amalgamated sums X_1 ⊔_Z X_2 along locally tame immersions, under M6 and M7.
The overall purpose is a category of tame spaces stable under finite limits, finite sums and gluings, suitable for a 'tame homotopy theory'.
